WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.960
Take aim, fire, and score.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.200
It's Duck Hunt.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:25.800
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:26.920
I'm Mike.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.140
I'm Sean Tresmond.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.360
I'm Joe Castu.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:36.240
All right. And Joe is joining us via satellite for this episode, so he might just be a little late,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.100
but for the most part, I think it'll work.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:45.260
Guys, today we're talking about Duck Hunt, that game that kind of came out in America like three times before it came out on NES.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:53.740
A lot of people don't know that, but Nintendo released it as kind of like this projector game that you shot at the wall with a real gun.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:59.420
And then it also went to the arcades and like the verse system thing, which was like a good thing Nintendo did.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.440
And then like... And you're saying you shot at the wall with a real gun.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:08.140
Well, yeah, that's dangerous, but believe it or not, you know, people love their guns.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:09.800
So they were okay with it.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:14.680
They, you know, completely remortgaged their house and that...

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.180
Yeah? Yeah, they just had to refinance the house after putting all those bullets in the wall.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:32.000
Okay. But more importantly, and what I was getting at, the success of the verse system version, that actually was kind of the catalyst for Nintendo to decide, like, the NES is worth sending that off to America.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:37.940
Like, Duck Hunt was the game that kind of convinced them we have like a viable stake in North America.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:46.180
Yeah. And I mean, I don't think it actually even did that well in Japan as opposed to how, you know, because we do love our guns over here.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.940
Yeah. And the game kind of does feel American.

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:50.900
Yeah. Right?

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.820
I mean, I don't know about those that like that ever present tree.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:59.340
I don't know if that like, that looks like an Asian tree to me.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:00.720
Yeah, there's no wind in this game.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.760
Yeah. You know, the tree never like, I don't even, what's an adjective to describe a tree?

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.820
Sway. Yeah, swaying.

00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.960
The tree never sways in the wind.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.020
Yeah. Okay, but let's just kind of do like first impressions.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.300
So yeah, Duck Hunt is as I remembered it.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.200
It was one of the games that I did play in my childhood on the NES.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.400
It's still fun to shoot stuff at a screen.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.660
It doesn't matter like how old the technology is that that does it.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.000
It's still because of its tactile nature.

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:39.500
And it's not just like D pad and two buttons.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.540
It's you're actually shooting at things.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.840
It doesn't really age at the same rate as the rest of these games.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.360
That's a good point. Joe?

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.980
So yeah, my initial impression was actually really, really positive.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:52.960
This, I also played this when I was a kid.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.820
And I kind of went into it thinking like, eh, it's probably not as good as I remember.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.620
But it kind of blew my mind.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.940
I was shocked at how well this old light gun game worked.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:05.640
And I had a blast.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.520
Okay, I kind of have a little bit of a different feel in terms of how well the game worked.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:17.900
Like, it is impressive that the zapper still works and that you can shoot the ducks and everything.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.520
But it is kind of, it's still restrictive in some ways.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.060
And there's, it's very easy to cheat.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.820
And it does become kind of really hard if you're too far away.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:29.720
But overall, I liked it.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.340
I think my first impression was just kind of like, this is, this is what I remember.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.080
It wasn't exciting.

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:40.200
It did, you know, like, it wasn't any better than when it was, than when I was a kid.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.820
So I think we should kind of just really quickly, even though it seems kind of obvious,

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.920
we should talk about the objective of the game.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.540
Does anybody kind of want to walk us through what you do?

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.600
All right, I'll give it a shot.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.600
So you are trying to get the dog.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.380
What? The version I played.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:07.620
Yes? That you're supposed to, you're supposed to shoot the dog, right?

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:09.380
Okay, I get it.

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.760
Yeah, yeah, yeah. I think everybody would love to shoot that dog.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:18.700
What Sean's referring to is sometimes if you don't shoot the birds, the dog will laugh at you.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.980
And this just brings up another useless trivia fact.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:27.060
In the, I think it's the verse system version or it's the original projector version.

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:28.540
Yeah, you can shoot the dog. You can shoot the dog.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:30.860
Yeah. In this one, you cannot shoot the dog.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.080
But people would love to do that.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.300
Joe, do you want to try and walk us through the objective of this game?

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:37.500
Yeah. I thought I'd like to do it as well.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.760
It was fun. So yeah, there's three different modes in this game.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.760
But the main objective of two of the modes is to shoot ducks.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.340
So, you know, you have the one duck version, which is kind of a lot easier than the other,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.040
where you're just kind of getting the hang of it.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.900
One duck kind of comes out, starts out pretty slow.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:59.660
You know, it zigs and zags, but, you know, it's just your simple target.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:01.780
Right. And then there's the two duck version.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:02.920
And then you have the two duck mode. Yeah.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:05.280
So the two duck version, which I think is much more fun.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.900
Obviously, you have two ducks coming out.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:10.420
They get faster a lot, a lot quicker.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.460
But yeah, you have your peripheral gun.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.880
So you're actually aiming in real life, which is what I thought kind of set this apart,

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:25.340
where it feels like you're kind of, it's kind of a skill based game on something you're doing in real life and not just like aiming within the game.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:31.400
I agree. And I think that's a good point to just jump in and kind of before we go too far into the rest of the game.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:34.840
I'd like to talk just really fast and to bring it up the zapper.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:41.480
I'd just like to talk about that because this is kind of the first game of a few that has the zapper involved in it.

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:46.380
And it's, first off, it's dramatically different than the Japanese version of the zapper.

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.260
The Japanese version of the zapper actually looks like a gun and even came with a holster.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:52.560
Yeah. So you can just like keep it in there.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:54.180
It was very Wild Westy.

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:57.500
Yeah. And then this one, the American version is actually, it's cool.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.080
It's futuristic. Yeah.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:09.540
One of the things that I've, I've heard people talk about when they talk about the change in the design of the zapper is that it's, it's like genre proof.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:17.560
Like it looks like it's a part of the system and not a themed item for a specific game that you're playing.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.040
Yeah. That's a great, yeah. That's actually a great way of describing that.

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:25.100
Yeah. So I would say that it was a good thing that they did that.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:29.380
I still would have thought it would be fun to shoot with like the, the little like six shooter thing.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:38.000
Kind of makes you wish that they did release like other, I'm sure there's a bootleg products, but like official versions of the zapper at the time, at least.

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:39.780
That's not useful anymore.

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:45.560
Well, my favorite part about the zapper is that is the, just how well the, just the two sites line up.

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:55.760
I mean, if you look down those two sites, it's then, and if you look down those two sites and you miss, the way I felt from everything I played, if you miss after looking down those two sites, you missed.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.020
Like I never. So you felt very rewarded and vindicated when you got a shot.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.260
Right. Like you felt like I deserve that shot.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:03.660
Like I shot that dump.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:07.140
Exactly. It never felt like when I missed, I could always tell like, yeah, I did miss.

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:10.460
Like it never felt like the game screwed up or like the game wasn't accurate enough.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:12.280
And then when I hit, I felt like, yeah, I hit.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.860
I got to wonder if I'm missing something. I got to wonder if I'm missing something there.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:15.720
You do play on a CRT.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:21.360
I didn't look through the, yeah, I did play on a CRT, but I didn't look through the crosshairs that you're talking about.

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.720
I kind of, I shot more with like a squinty eye.

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:31.800
Right. So if you look through those crosshairs, that's, I mean, what I found is like you, that's how you can know you're being as accurate as possible.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:39.240
See, I found, I always had, um, the way that I played the game is you just hold it up to the screen and then you don't miss.

00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:40.600
It doesn't matter. You will never miss.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:43.780
Yeah. It doesn't matter what, like how the actual crosshair lines up.

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:47.920
That's actually a great way, uh, Sean, to feel really good at a video game.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:51.500
It's just to, I've never felt better about a video game than when I cheat.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:08:02.780
That's true. It is very easy to cheat in this game, but I, but I will say it's, it's kind of interesting that they built in a, an anti-cheating device, even though it's still so easy to just hold it up to the cheat, to the screen.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:13.680
But, uh, if you were to just shine a flashlight in the light gun, the way the light, real quick, the way the light gun works is that when you pull the trigger, the ducks turn into white squares.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:17.040
And if the gun senses those white squares, it's a hit, which is kind of genius.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:21.840
Right. So if you could, if you could just shine a flashlight in there, it would always sense it as a hit.

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:28.580
So what it does is when you pull the trigger, it actually has one quick black frame and then one quick white frame.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:34.580
Oh yeah. So if, if it doesn't sense the black frame first, it knows you're not pointing it at the screen and it automatically counts it as a miss.

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:36.740
Wow. That's really cool.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:41.120
Which is also why it doesn't work on like HD TVs because there's a little bit of lag.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:42.980
One of the very first frame perfect inputs.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:45.340
Exactly. Wow.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.020
Oh, and one real quick thing.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:49.960
I have to give a shout out to my zapper because it's not the gray one.

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.180
I actually had the orange one and I really.

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:54.680
I think that was, I think that was, um, more common.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.800
Really? Because they. I actually don't know if I've ever seen a gray one.

00:08:58.300 --> 00:08:59.620
Yeah. I've only seen the orange one.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:01.560
Because I think during. It's more of the NES colors.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:10.420
Yeah. I think during the eighties, um, like some law was passed that all mock guns had to have that orange bit.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:12.740
Oh, right. Like, and they would have that red tip too.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:13.900
Trying to wear that. Yeah.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.860
So I think this was them just going like, we're going to make the whole thing orange.

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:19.200
All right. So that, that's it for the zapper.

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.400
Uh, but let's talk about the game modes.

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:25.880
First, let's just start very fast with the, uh, first mode, which is just the one duck.

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:29.320
Uh, anybody have any strong feelings about this?

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.280
Strong feelings about one duck?

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.860
Uh, well, actually that's what the mode is called too.

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.020
It says like one duck and then the second book, two ducks.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:42.680
And it's like, okay. So, so my only particularly strong feelings about this is, I mean, first of all, it's very easy.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.920
This, this is the mode that you use to like, oh, to figure out what the game is to, to practice.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.460
It's definitely like a learning mode.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:53.000
And you'll see this kind of in almost every NES game that has like a mode A and a mode B.

00:09:53.140 --> 00:09:56.420
Right. It's like mode A is more like you learning the game.

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:57.480
It's a little more friendlier.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.960
And then like mode B is kind of like, okay, you want to challenge.

00:10:01.340 --> 00:10:02.500
This is what you get. Right.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:08.320
But what I didn't know when I was a kid, someone just told me, you know, recently before I played

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:13.900
it this time is that a second, in one duck mode, a second player can control the ducks,

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.440
which blew my mind.

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:17.660
I did not, I never knew that you could do that.

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.480
I think I remember doing that.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:20.620
Did you have someone to do that with?

00:10:20.780 --> 00:10:24.660
Yes. Yeah. So I actually played this at a friend's house and I think that might be part of why I'm

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:29.020
so positive on it is playing this with a friend and competing and going back and forth was

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.060
a, it was just, it just didn't get old because it's just that, you know, nature of competition.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:39.620
Um, but yeah, so we, we controlled the one duck and I will say for the first, maybe 10 rounds,

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:44.200
it's kind of useless to control the duck because he moves so slow anyways that.

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:47.460
What is your, yeah. What's your like range of control on the duck?

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:51.100
Cause the duck in the game already has like some pretty interesting diagonal paths.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:54.460
Right. I can't imagine you being able to pull off with the NES controller.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:59.340
You, so you, you can, um, the only thing is you can't change the speed, but you can change

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:03.360
the direction on like whatever six points or whatever the, whatever it moves.

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:09.080
Right. Um, but so once it gets to maybe around 10 and it starts to speed up, that's when suddenly

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:12.660
the second player feels like they can screw over the first player a little more easily

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:13.660
because it's moving faster.

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:17.300
So like I would do little things like I would try and stay at the top of the screen.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:20.080
So when it was ready to fly away, it would just fly away immediately.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:21.900
It wouldn't have to cover the distance of the screen.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.580
Right. Or sometimes I would just try and hang out behind the tree.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:26.020
So it was harder to see the duck.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:30.420
It's only little, little things that you can, that you can really do, but it's, it's,

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:33.480
it's a fun, you know, it's a fun aside from the rest of the game.

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:37.160
Okay. Uh, Sean, any feelings on one duck or should we move on to two duck?

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:40.660
I think everything that can be said about one duck has just been said.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:41.540
That's, that's beautiful.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:43.200
And so let's move on to two ducks.

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:49.200
Kind of the real mode here is we're all alluding to.

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:53.380
I definitely feel like this is a duck hunt at its purest.

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.940
I think if you did three ducks, that would be like way too much.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:00.340
Yeah. Uh, and especially considering you only have three shots too, it'd just be like a major

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:05.660
challenge. But I really respect kind of like the randomness of the pattern of the ducks too.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:09.140
Like even right off the bat, even though they're not going too fast, you still have,

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:12.760
there's a bit of a learning curve there because you have to kind of follow both ducks while

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:14.240
still only being able to shoot at one.

00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:17.360
I mean, was anybody able to shoot both ducks down at the same time?

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:19.340
Is that even possible? I don't think they overlap.

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:22.380
So, Oh, you mean that with one shot, right?

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:26.300
With one shot. Like I just, I was trying to do that, but it wasn't happening.

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:29.920
Like there was never a time where I could perfectly hit them both.

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:33.700
I, because of the technology, I don't think that like, it would have just

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:34.680
any information. The one.

00:12:34.980 --> 00:12:40.500
Yeah. Right. Because, well, that's another, uh, thing about the, not to get back into the gun, but it,

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:41.680
No, I love it. Keep going.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:45.160
When there's two ducks, um, it'll do black frame.

00:12:45.620 --> 00:12:49.680
Then one, the first duck will light up in one frame and the second duck will light up

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:50.500
in a different frame.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:52.460
How does it know? Oh, I, I, I, sorry.

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:53.940
I almost asked a really dumb question.

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:55.420
I get it. Yeah.

00:12:55.420 --> 00:13:00.460
So it just depends on which, which frame it senses the light and it knows which duck you hit.

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:03.160
Did anybody play this game like for the high score?

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:05.660
Like was anyone trying to chase a certain score or anything?

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:08.160
When, when I was playing, I really didn't care about the score.

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:11.740
I just wanted to keep like seeing how much faster the ducks would get.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:13.080
I never really checked on my score.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:14.860
I couldn't even report to you what my high score is.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:15.900
No. Yeah. Same thing.

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:20.880
I don't think that just because of like what we grew up with, we even, we do even,

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.060
do you pay attention to scores in any game?

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:24.900
I rarely pay attention to scores.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:26.640
Especially when they're like that big.

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:28.360
If I was on a board in a new game, maybe.

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:29.860
Yeah. Like I'll pay attention to the score.

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:34.980
But in these games, I, I, no, I was never, and I think like I was also never good enough

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:36.120
to care. Yeah, exactly.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:44.000
So I wouldn't have. I mean, you can, you can even like, I know this is going out of, out of episode, but if

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.820
you look at Mario, I mean like there are scores in Mario, but did you ever care about

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:51.480
your score in Mario? It's often, they just like, it's an arcade thing.

00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:55.900
Yeah. You know, like the idea of like having a high score, putting your initials in the cabinet,

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:59.660
like that was something that was still like, even though that's really only appropriate

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:03.700
at the arcade, consoles did it as a way to kind of like keep that.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:04.960
Bring your friends over. Right, right.

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:07.180
And keep that arcade experience at the house.

